# The sentinel that runs .github/workflows/ledger-setup-msvc.yml.
#
# TOUCH THIS FILE AND COMMIT IT TO INSTALL BLENDER on the build PC. The
# workflow keeps its MSVC name and its MSVC sentinel; this is a second
# trigger path on the same file, because a third near-copy of a
# checkout/bootstrap/install/verify/commit workflow is how the PATH
# bootstrap ended up existing three times with the third one shortened.
#
# IT LIVES HERE, beside SETUP-MSVC, and not in production/mesh-reports/
# where its report goes. mesh-reports is an OUTPUT directory: a trigger
# inside one is how a run's own output re-fires the workflow that made it,
# or how a cleanup quietly deletes the trigger. The REPORT belongs there,
# next to the mesh machine report that names Blender as the missing piece.
#
# WHY. tools/meshgen's local backend refused on JAFAR-DESKTOP with
# "backend local: NO (1 of 2 requirements met)" - see
# production/mesh-reports/mesh-machine-report.txt. The one missing
# requirement is Blender. Nothing else about that backend is missing and
# 84 GB is free.
#
# Blender is free and GPL from blender.org. No purchase, no account, and
# none is used. Authorized by Jafar 2026-09-01: "can't you installed
# blender like oyu installed MSVC".
#
# IDEMPOTENT, AND IT SAYS WHICH. If Blender is already there the run
# reports outcome=already-present and changes nothing, so touching this
# file again is free. The report it writes is
# production/mesh-reports/blender-setup.txt, committed by the run itself
# and naming the commit it was measured on.
#
# NOTE that touching this also re-runs the MSVC step, which is idempotent
# too: with the required set already complete it prints ALREADY COMPLETE
# and exits in seconds. That is a re-measurement, not a change.
#
# One line per run, appended, so this file is also the setup log.
run 1 - 2026-09-01 - install Blender, the only requirement tools/meshgen's
local backend is missing. The version is DISCOVERED, never guessed: the run
reads https://download.blender.org/release/, takes the newest LTS line it
finds from a named preference list, takes the highest Windows x64 MSI in
that line, and prints the URL and version it chose. If the index cannot be
read it stops and says so rather than falling back to a hardcoded URL that
404s the day Blender ships a patch.

run 2 - 2026-09-01 - run 1 reached msiexec and failed with 1603, which under
a silent install is rights: the runner service cannot write to Program Files.
Everything before it worked, including discovering 4.5.13 from the release
index and downloading 360 MB. This run does not install at all. It extracts
the portable zip to C:\LedgerTools\blender, which needs no elevation, and it
tests whether it can write there BEFORE spending the download. Verification
calls meshgen's own probe rather than checking a path, so an extraction
landing somewhere the prop batch does not look cannot pass.
